{"input": "What did the Professor think about the results?", "answers": ["The professor noted that the results were better than he expected and that the MSG did nothing. He also noted that adding French and Spanish to the multilingual English model hurt the model."], "length": 14016, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The Professor seemed to be generally pleased with the results, but also acknowledged that there were still some issues to be addressed, particularly with the MSG problem and the language dependency. The Professor noted that the TIMIT network was still the best, but that the results were not yet conclusive and that further work was needed to improve the performance of the system. The Professor also expressed some concerns about the potential for the MLP to not help in certain conditions, and suggested that the focus should be on improving the training targets and data rather than trying to combine multiple features and MLPs."}
{"input": "Summarize the meeting", "answers": ["The meeting began with the team catching up the professor, who had missed the previous meeting. Then, the team moved onto talking about echo cancelling techniques. They were trying to get the voice of other people from the primary person's microphone. Then, the team discussed how they could improve the performance on the Aurora task by playing around with the model. The team ended the meeting with various comments on different topics."], "length": 8183, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The meeting discussed various aspects of speech recognition, including the use of Linear Discriminant Analysis (LDA) filters, downsampling, and noise removal techniques. The group also talked about the challenges of getting clean channels for close-miked recordings, particularly in the context of the Aurora and ICSI-OGI tasks. They also discussed the idea of using spectral subtraction and adaptive filtering to remove background speech from close-miked recordings. Additionally, the group touched on the topic of feature extraction, including the use of mel-cepstral features and the potential for using other features such as sonorant detection. They also discussed the idea of using simpler models, such as those with fewer Gaussians, and the potential for using batch mode to run Matlab scripts."}
{"input": "Summarize the whole meeting.", "answers": ["This meeting was about the detailed design. Firstly, User Interface presented on the prototype, including the shape, the size of the remote and the user interface including the buttons and the scrolling wheel. Next, the group had an evaluation criterion on the prototype. They scored an overall six point five average on the prototype and thought they had done well on it. Then, the group had a discussion about project finance. The group decided not to have a sample sensor and the locator because they had a production cost of 12.5 Euros. Lastly, the group had a discussion about the project process, including the use of a pen, the whiteboard and the PowerPoint. Also, the group was satisfied with Project Manager's leadership and Project Manager also mentioned that their costs were within the budget as well as some admin work."], "length": 9366, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The meeting was a detailed design meeting for a new remote control product, where the team discussed the prototype presentation, evaluation criteria, and production costs. The team evaluated the product's design, features, and functionality, and made decisions on which features to keep and which to remove due to budget constraints. They also discussed the project's process, identified areas for improvement, and provided feedback on the meeting process. The meeting was productive, and the team was able to complete the evaluation and make decisions on the product's design and features."}
