{"input": "Summarize the whole meeting.", "answers": ["This meeting was about the functional design of the remote control. Firstly, the Project Manager mentioned new requirements including one recognizable button in the middle, the handy design, rubber materials and they would not use teletext. Next, Marketing presented on functional requirements including a fancy look, ease of use, and the LCD as well as speech recognition. Then, Industrial Designer introduced technical design and working design on remote interfaces. Lastly, User Interface presented on component design and the group decided to adopt a user-centered approach to reduce the main buttons and put a big circle in the middle with the power button, channel, and volume buttons."], "length": 11299, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The meeting was about designing a remote control for a new D_V_D_ player. The team discussed various aspects of the remote, including the layout of buttons, the use of a LCD screen, and the design of the front panel. They also talked about the importance of making the remote user-friendly and familiar, while also incorporating new features such as a LCD screen and voice recognition. The team decided to put the LCD screen at the top of the remote, with the most important buttons, such as power, channel, and volume, located in a central area. They also discussed the use of a menu button and the settings for brightness and contrast. The meeting concluded with the team agreeing to go their separate ways to work on their individual tasks, with the understanding that they would reconvene later to finalize the design."}
{"input": "Summarize the whole meeting.", "answers": ["The meeting was mainly about conceptual design. In the beginning, the group focused on industrial components: energy, material and interface. The industrial designer preferred traditional batteries, titanium and simple push buttons so that they would have enough money for speech recognition. After that, the group talked about LCD display and locating methods. The second topic was user interface concept, which was basically a good-looking design with few buttons. The group decided to change the colour of the assembly at an extra charge to meet special needs. To make a smart controller, they mentioned speech recognition and user identification. When it came to trend watching, the marketing put forward three noteworthy aspects - being fancy, technologically innovative and useful. The group agreed to follow the trend but the specific design was not decided."], "length": 6952, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The meeting was a conceptual design meeting for a new remote control. The team discussed various aspects of the remote control, including its design, functionality, and features. They considered different materials, such as titanium, rubber, and plastic, and weighed the pros and cons of each. The team also discussed the importance of a \"fancy\" look and feel, with some members suggesting that the remote control should be designed to resemble a fruit, such as a banana. They also considered the use of speech recognition and a simple button layout, and decided to explore these options further. Additionally, the team discussed the trend of using fruit and vegetable-inspired designs in consumer products and how this could be applied to the remote control. They also decided to keep the LCD display and use a simple battery design. Overall, the meeting was focused on brainstorming and exploring different design options for the remote control."}
{"input": "What were the problems encountered with SmartKom system?", "answers": ["The translation of SmartKom to english is in its final stages. The synthesis module will be the last one to do, after the english syntax trees are completed. The system is still buggy and unstable, but it will soon be ready for a demonstration."], "length": 12646, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The SmartKom system encountered several problems, including instability, requiring a lot of workarounds, and being brittle, requiring 17 modules to be changed before it could be used. Additionally, the system was not language-independent, except for parsing and generation, and the syntax trees were simple, with most sentences being represented in a single tree."}
